    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nAfter `:restart`, the buffer that was current at restart time is restored with an empty\n`'filetype'`, so it has no syntax or treesitter highlighting. `:setf` does not fix it;\n`:e!` does.\n\nOnly that one buffer is affected. Files of the same filetype opened afterwards are fine.\nAlso reproducible with `:restart!`: the first file opened afterwards is affected.\n\nRoot cause (traced with `-V20` and autocmd instrumentation):\n\n1. `:restart` restores the session by sourcing a temp `:mksession` file from a `UIEnter`\n   once-autocmd (`runtime/lua/vim/_core/server.lua`, `ex_session_restart()`).\n2. The session file runs `edit <file>`, nested inside the UIEnter autocmd batch.\n3. If LSP setup lazy-loads on `BufReadPre` (the first real file read in the new server,\n   e.g. lazy.nvim loading nvim-lspconfig/mason-lspconfig), `vim.lsp.enable()` runs while\n   the buffer is mid-read, before filetype detection (`BufReadPost`).\n4. `vim.lsp.enable()` runs `vim.cmd.doautoall('nvim.lsp.enable FileType')`\n   (`runtime/lua/vim/lsp.lua:655`). `ex_doautoall()` executes the event for the current\n   buffer unconditionally (`src/nvim/autocmd.c:1210`), and executing the FileType event\n   sets `b_did_filetype = true` (`src/nvim/autocmd.c:1669`), even though `'filetype'` is\n   still empty.\n5. Because the read is nested inside the UIEnter batch, the flag is not reset afterwards\n   (the reset at `src/nvim/autocmd.c:1774` only runs for the outermost batch).\n6. At `BufReadPost`, the `filetypedetect` callback in `runtime/filetype.lua` computes the\n   correct filetype but applies it with `:setf`, which is a no-op when `did_filetype()`\n   is true (`src/nvim/ex_docmd.c:8002`). `'filetype'` stays empty permanently.\n\nInstrumented run shows `did_filetype() == 1` already at `BufReadPost` of the restored\nbuffer while `filetype` is empty. With `nvim --clean` and no `lsp.enable()` the restored\nbuffer gets its filetype correctly.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n`minimal.lua`:\n\n```lua\nvim.lsp.config('dummy', { cmd = { 'false' }, filetypes = { 'markdown' } })\nvim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('BufReadPre', {\n  once = true,\n  callback = function()\n    -- Simulates lazy-loaded LSP setup (nvim-lspconfig/mason-lspconfig) running\n    -- during the session-restore edit. vim.lsp.enable() runs\n    -- \"doautoall FileType\", which marks the mid-read buffer as did_filetype.\n    vim.lsp.enable('dummy')\n  end,\n})\n```\n\n```\nnvim --clean -u minimal.lua\n:e foo.md\n:restart\n:set ft?        \" filetype= (empty), no highlighting\n:e bar.md       \" other files of the same filetype are fine\n```\n\nThe nesting inside an autocmd batch is essential; this non-restart variant also\nreproduces it:\n\n```lua\nvim.lsp.config('dummy', { cmd = { 'false' }, filetypes = { 'markdown' } })\nvim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('BufReadPre', {\n  once = true,\n  callback = function() vim.lsp.enable('dummy') end,\n})\nvim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('User', {\n  pattern = 'DoEdit',\n  callback = function() vim.cmd.edit('foo.md') end,\n})\n```\n\n```\nnvim --clean -u minimal.lua\n:doautocmd User DoEdit\n:set ft?        \" filetype= (empty)\n```\n\n(A plain `:e foo.md` after startup does not reproduce it, because `b_did_filetype` is\nreset when the outermost autocmd batch ends.)\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nFiletype detection works for buffers restored by `:restart`; highlighting is preserved.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.13.0-dev-1469+g16a1d203e9-Homebrew\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nN/A (`:restart` is Nvim-only)\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nmacOS 27.0 (arm64)\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nghostty\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-ghostty\n\n### Installation\n\nhomebrew (HEAD)",
