            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "I tried the linked PR and it doesn't solve the issue. This issue seems also relevant to an old question I had [here](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/33702#discussion_r2128539018). It's tied specifically to `doautoall` command in `vim.lsp.enable` (I only started having this problem when it was added to `vim.lsp.enable`).\n\nThe following patch (no2 of the possible fixes mentioned by OP)\n```diff\ndiff --git a/src/nvim/autocmd.c b/src/nvim/autocmd.c\nindex 65d1543f96..e51b930078 100644\n--- a/src/nvim/autocmd.c\n+++ b/src/nvim/autocmd.c\n@@ -1665,7 +1665,7 @@ bool apply_autocmds_group(event_T event, char *fname, char *fname_io, bool force\n   nesting++;  // see matching decrement below\n \n   // Remember that FileType was triggered.  Used for did_filetype().\n-  if (event == EVENT_FILETYPE) {\n+  if (event == EVENT_FILETYPE && *curbuf->b_p_ft != NUL) {\n     curbuf->b_did_filetype = true;\n   }\n```\nseems to rectify both this issue as well as my old question. Not fluent at all in C and Neovim internals however, so I'll defer any judgement to more knowledgeable people. Is there any downside to only setting `b_did_filetype = true` when the filetype of the buffer is not empty? Is there a potential case where it has to be set even when filetype of buffer is empty? Because with this patch the `BufRead` autocmd in `filetype.lua` is able to correctly set the filetype for the first buffer via `setf`.\n```lua\nverbose set ft?   filetype=lua\n\tLast set from ~/neovim/share/nvim/runtime/filetype.lua line 28\n```",
